            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Search for an Ocean Route to India</h3>\n<p>The capture of Constantinople by the Ottoman Turks in <strong>1453</strong> closed the traditional overland trade routes linking Europe and Asia. Under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator, Portuguese sailors explored the West African coast. In 1488, <strong>Bartolomeu Dias</strong> rounded the southern tip of Africa, christening it the 'Cape of Good Hope'.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Vasco da Gama's Historic Arrival (1498)</h3>\n<p>On <strong>May 20, 1498</strong>, Vasco da Gama arrived at Calicut aboard the flagship <em>São Gabriel</em>, guided by Gujarati navigator <strong>Abdul Majid</strong>. He was cordially received by the Hindu hereditary ruler, the <strong>Zamorin (Samutiri)</strong>, despite fierce opposition from Arab traders who dominated Indian Ocean commerce. Vasco da Gama returned to Lisbon with spices that yielded sixty times the cost of the entire expedition.</p>\n<p>In 1502, Vasco da Gama returned on a second voyage, establishing factory settlements at <strong>Cochin</strong> (first Portuguese factory in India, 1500/1503 by Pedro Álvares Cabral and Albuquerque) and <strong>Cannanore</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Francisco de Almeida &amp; The Blue Water Policy (1505–1509)</h3>\n<p>Appointed as the first Viceroy of Portuguese India, Almeida sought to secure Portuguese trade by establishing maritime naval supremacy rather than conquering vast continental territories—a doctrine termed the <strong>Blue Water Policy (A Política do Mar Oceano)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Cartaz System:</strong> A compulsory naval pass issued by the Portuguese crown. Any Asian vessel trading in the Indian Ocean without a Cartaz faced confiscation of cargo and crew enslavement.</li>\n  <li><strong>Battle of Chaul (1508):</strong> A combined fleet of the Mamluk Sultanate of Egypt and the Sultanate of Gujarat defeated Almeida's son, Lourenço.</li>\n  <li><strong>Battle of Diu (1509):</strong> Almeida crushed the allied naval forces of Egypt, Gujarat, and the Zamorin, securing Portuguese maritime dominance for over a century.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Afonso de Albuquerque &amp; Territorial Imperialism (1509–1515)</h3>\n<p>Considered the true architect of the Portuguese Empire in the East (<em>Estado da Índia</em>), Albuquerque pursued strategic choke points:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Capture of Goa (1510):</strong> Albuquerque conquered Goa from <strong>Ismail Adil Shah</strong> of Bijapur. Goa provided an impregnable natural harbor, naval shipyards, and control over the vital Arabian horse trade.</li>\n  <li><strong>Strategic Fortresses:</strong> Captured Malacca (1511, controlling the Malacca Straits) and Ormuz (1515, controlling the Persian Gulf).</li>\n  <li><strong>Social Policies:</strong> Encouraged Portuguese soldiers to marry native Indian women to create a permanent settled Eurasian population, and prohibited <strong>Sati</strong> within Goa.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>5. Nino da Cunha &amp; Portuguese Expansion (1529–1538)</h3>\n<p>In <strong>1530</strong>, Nino da Cunha shifted the Portuguese capital from Cochin to Goa. He acquired <strong>Bassein (1534)</strong> and <strong>Diu (1535)</strong> through treaties with Bahadur Shah of Gujarat. The Portuguese also established thriving trading settlements along the Coromandel Coast at <strong>San Thome (Madras)</strong> and in Bengal at <strong>Hooghly</strong>.</p>\n",
