            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Factors in the Decline of Portuguese Power</h3>\n<p>By the 17th century, Portuguese dominance in the Indian subcontinent eroded rapidly due to several factors:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Religious Intolerance &amp; Inquisition:</strong> The introduction of the Goa Inquisition (1560) and aggressive proselytization alienated both Hindu and Muslim rulers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Piracy &amp; Hostilities:</strong> Portuguese settlements (particularly in the Bay of Bengal and Chittagong) engaged in piracy and slave trafficking. In <strong>1632</strong>, Mughal Emperor <strong>Shah Jahan</strong> ordered <strong>Qasim Khan</strong> (Governor of Bengal) to besiege and sack the Portuguese stronghold of <strong>Hooghly</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Discovery of Brazil:</strong> The economic potential of Brazil diverted Portuguese colonization efforts and capital westward across the Atlantic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Loss of Strategic Forts:</strong> The Persian Safavids, aided by British ships, expelled the Portuguese from <strong>Hormuz in 1622</strong>. The Dutch systematically seized Portuguese possessions in Ceylon (1658) and Malabar (1663).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Dutch East India Company (VOC - 1602)</h3>\n<p>Formed in <strong>1602</strong> as the <em>Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie (VOC)</em> by merging competing commercial enterprises, it was the world's first publicly traded company issuing shares. The Dutch government granted the VOC a 21-year monopoly with sovereign powers to wage war, build fortresses, and conclude treaties.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Dutch Factories and Trade in India</h3>\n<p>The Dutch established their earliest Indian factories across strategic trade coasts:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Masulipatnam (1605):</strong> The first Dutch factory in India, established on the Andhra coast with permission from the Sultan of Golconda.</li>\n  <li><strong>Petapuli (Nizampatnam, 1606):</strong> Early factory on the Coromandel Coast.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pulicat (1610):</strong> The chief Dutch administrative center on the Coromandel Coast; defended by <strong>Fort Geldria</strong>; minted the famous gold coin known as the <strong>Pagoda</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Surat (1616):</strong> Prime textile and indigo procurement center in Western India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chinsurah (1653):</strong> Chief Dutch factory in Bengal (defended by <strong>Fort Gustavus</strong>), dealing in silk, saltpetre, and opium.</li>\n  <li><strong>Negapatam (1658):</strong> Captured from the Portuguese; replaced Pulicat as the Dutch Coromandel capital in 1690.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Anglo-Dutch Rivalry &amp; The Battle of Bedara (1759)</h3>\n<p>Following the <strong>Amboyna Massacre (1623)</strong> in the Moluccas, the Dutch and English concluded an informal mutual sphere of influence. However, in 1759, during the Seven Years' War, Nawab Mir Jafar secretly invited the Dutch from Batavia to counter Robert Clive's growing dominance in Bengal. In November 1759, British troops led by <strong>Colonel Francis Forde</strong> decisively routed the Dutch army and naval fleet at the <strong>Battle of Bedara (Battle of Chinsurah)</strong>. The Dutch signed a humiliating treaty, abandoning all military ambitions in India.</p>\n",
