            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Genesis of the English East India Company</h3>\n<p>Formed in 1599 by a group of prominent London merchants known as the <strong>Merchant Adventurers</strong>, the company received its historic Royal Charter from <strong>Queen Elizabeth I on December 31, 1600</strong> under the title <em>'The Governor and Company of Merchants of London Trading into the East Indies'</em>. It was granted a monopoly of 15 years to trade across the lands east of the Cape of Good Hope and west of the Straits of Magellan. In 1609, King James I granted a fresh charter extending the monopoly indefinitely.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Missions of Hawkins &amp; Sir Thomas Roe</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Captain William Hawkins (1608):</strong> Commanded the ship <em>Hector</em>, docking at Surat in August 1608. He traveled to Agra in 1609, presenting King James I's letter to Emperor <strong>Jahangir</strong>. Hawkins, fluent in Turkish, was granted a mansab of 400 and given the title <em>English Khan</em>, but Portuguese Jesuit intrigue prevented him from securing permission for a Surat factory.</li>\n  <li><strong>Battle of Swally (1612):</strong> Captain <strong>Thomas Best</strong> in the <em>Red Dragon</em> decisively defeated a superior Portuguese fleet at Swally Hole off Surat. Convinced of English naval prowess, Jahangir issued a farman in early <strong>1613</strong> permitting the English to build their first permanent factory at <strong>Surat</strong> under Thomas Aldworth.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sir Thomas Roe (1615–1618):</strong> Accredited royal ambassador of King James I to Jahangir's court. Roe resided at the court for three years, securing imperial farmans granting the Company full freedom to trade and establish factories at Agra, Ahmedabad, and Bharuch.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Establishment of Early English Settlements</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Masulipatnam (1611):</strong> The first English factory in Southern India, established on the Coromandel coast with permission from Golconda authorities.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Golden Farman of Golconda (1632):</strong> The Sultan of Golconda granted the English the right to trade freely in his ports for a fixed annual payment of 500 pagodas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Madras &amp; Fort St. George (1639):</strong> <strong>Francis Day</strong> obtained the coastal strip of Madraspatnam on lease from the Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka (representative of the Chandragiri Raja). Here, the Company constructed <strong>Fort St. George</strong>, which became the first fortified settlement of the English in India and replaced Masulipatnam as the headquarters on the Coromandel Coast in 1652.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hariharpur &amp; Balasore (1633):</strong> The first English factories in Eastern India (Odisha).</li>\n</ul>\n",
