            "title": "Acquisition of Bombay, Calcutta & Farrukhsiyar's Royal Farman",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Acquisition and Development of Bombay</h3>\n<p>In <strong>1661</strong>, the Portuguese Crown granted the archipelago of <strong>Bombay</strong> to King Charles II of England as part of the dowry for his marriage to Princess <strong>Catherine of Braganza</strong>. Finding the remote islands administratively cumbersome, Charles II transferred Bombay to the East India Company in <strong>1668</strong> for an annual nominal lease of <strong>£10</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Gerald Aungier (1669–1677):</strong> Appointed Governor of Bombay and President of Surat, Aungier drained swamps, established law courts, built fortifications, set up a mint, and welcomed skilled Gujarati artisans and Parsi traders. He declared Bombay 'the city which by God's assistance is intended to be built'. In 1687, the Company shifted its Western presidency headquarters from Surat to Bombay.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Job Charnock &amp; The Founding of Calcutta (1690)</h3>\n<p>Following military clashes with Bengal Governor Shaista Khan in the 1680s, English agent <strong>Job Charnock</strong> signed a treaty in <strong>August 1690</strong>, establishing a factory at the village of <strong>Sutanuti</strong> on the banks of the Hooghly river.</p>\n<p>In <strong>1698</strong>, with the approval of Subahdar Azim-us-Shan (grandson of Aurangzeb), the English purchased the zamindari rights of three villages—<strong>Sutanuti, Gobindapur, and Kalikata</strong>—from the Sabarna Roy Choudhury family for ₹1,200.</p>\n<p>The fortified factory was named <strong>Fort William</strong> in 1700 (after King William III of England), and <strong>Sir Charles Eyre</strong> was appointed its first President. Fort William became the seat of the Bengal Presidency.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Farrukhsiyar's Royal Farman (1717): The Magna Carta of EIC</h3>\n<p>In 1715, an English diplomatic mission led by <strong>John Surman</strong> visited the court of Mughal Emperor <strong>Farrukhsiyar</strong> in Delhi. The mission included surgeon <strong>William Hamilton</strong>, who successfully treated the Emperor of a painful swelling. Pleased with the cure, Farrukhsiyar issued three imperial farmans in <strong>1717</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Bengal:</strong> Freedom from all inland custom duties on payment of a consolidated annual fee of ₹3,000. The Company was authorized to issue <strong>Dastaks</strong> (transit passes) for the movement of goods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Additional Land:</strong> Permission to rent 38 villages surrounding Calcutta.</li>\n  <li><strong>Coinage:</strong> Coins minted by the Company at Bombay were made legal tender throughout the Mughal Empire.</li>\n  <li><strong>Surat:</strong> Exemption from custom duties at Surat for an annual payment of ₹10,000.</li>\n  <li><strong>Hyderabad:</strong> Retention of existing trade privileges and duty exemptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>British historian <strong>C.R. Wilson</strong> famously hailed this charter as the <em>'Magna Carta of the Company'</em> because it provided a formal legal foundation for the Company's commercial and political dominance in India.</p>\n",
