            "moduleId": "m06",
            "moduleNumber": 6,
            "title": "The Carnatic Wars (1746–1763 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Background: Anglo-French Rivalry in the Deccan</h3>\n<p>The Coromandel Coast and its hinterland (the <strong>Carnatic</strong>, technically a subah of the Nizam of Hyderabad) became the primary battleground between the English (headquartered at Madras) and the French (headquartered at Pondicherry). Their conflicts, spanning nearly two decades (1746–1763), are known as the <strong>Carnatic Wars</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. First Carnatic War (1746–1748)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cause:</strong> The outbreak of the <strong>War of the Austrian Succession (1740–1748)</strong> in Europe. English commodore Curtis Barnett seized French merchant ships. In retaliation, French Governor-General <strong>Joseph François Dupleix</strong>, aided by <strong>La Bourdonnais</strong> (Governor of Mauritius), besieged and captured <strong>Madras in 1746</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Battle of St. Thome (Adyar River, 1746):</strong> Nawab Anwar-ud-din of Carnatic sent an army of 10,000 under Mahfuz Khan to liberate Madras. A small, highly disciplined French force of only 230 Europeans and 700 trained Indian sepoys commanded by <strong>Captain Paradise</strong> routed the Nawab's massive cavalry. This battle demonstrated that a small, disciplined European-drilled infantry with modern muskets and rapid-firing field artillery could effortlessly shatter large, unwieldy traditional Indian armies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The <strong>Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748)</strong> concluded the European war: Madras was returned to the English in exchange for the French regaining Louisbourg in North America.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Second Carnatic War (1749–1754)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Genesis:</strong> In 1748, the deaths of Nizam-ul-Mulk of Hyderabad and the succession contest in Carnatic created a double succession crisis:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Hyderabad:</strong> Nasir Jung (son) vs <strong>Muzaffar Jung</strong> (grandson).</li>\n      <li><strong>Carnatic:</strong> Anwar-ud-din (Nawab) vs <strong>Chanda Sahib</strong> (brother-in-law of former Nawab Dost Ali).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Dupleix's Masterstroke:</strong> Dupleix allied with Muzaffar Jung and Chanda Sahib. At the <strong>Battle of Ambur (1749)</strong>, they defeated and killed Anwar-ud-din. Chanda Sahib became Nawab of Carnatic, and Muzaffar Jung became Nizam, rewarding Dupleix with vast revenues, lands around Pondicherry, and appointing him honorary governor of all Mughal territories south of the Krishna River. French commander <strong>Charles de Bussy</strong> was stationed at Hyderabad with a subsidiary French force.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clive's Heroic Defense of Arcot (1751):</strong> While Chanda Sahib and French troops besieged Anwar-ud-din's son, <strong>Muhammad Ali</strong>, at Tiruchirappalli, a young English clerk-turned-soldier, <strong>Robert Clive</strong>, proposed a daring diversion: storming <strong>Arcot</strong> (Chanda Sahib's capital) with only 210 men. Clive captured Arcot and withstood a ferocious 53-day siege by Chanda Sahib's son Raza Sahib. This broke French momentum; Muhammad Ali was relieved and installed as Nawab of Carnatic, while Chanda Sahib was captured and executed by the Raja of Tanjore.</li>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Pondicherry (1754):</strong> Disheartened by massive expenditures, the French government recalled Dupleix in 1754 and sent <strong>Charles Godeheu</strong>, who signed the Treaty of Pondicherry, pledging non-interference in the affairs of native princes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Third Carnatic War (1758–1763) &amp; The Battle of Wandiwash</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cause:</strong> The outbreak of the <strong>Seven Years' War (1756–1763)</strong> in Europe. The French sent <strong>Count de Lally</strong> with large forces. Lally captured Fort St. David (1758) but committed tactical blunders: he recalled Bussy from Hyderabad (enabling British Colonel Forde to conquer the Northern Circars) and launched a failed siege of Madras.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Battle of Wandiwash (January 22, 1760):</strong> British General <strong>Sir Eyre Coote</strong> decisively smashed the French army under Count de Lally and Bussy at Wandiwash (Tamil Nadu). Bussy was taken prisoner.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fall of Pondicherry (1761):</strong> Eyre Coote besieged and captured Pondicherry, razing its fortifications. Lally returned to France in disgrace and was guillotined for treason.</li>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Paris (1763):</strong> Restored Pondicherry, Chandernagore, Mahe, and Karaikal to the French, but under strict condition that they could never be fortified or maintain troops. The French were permanently relegated to minor commercial enclaves.</li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Carnatic Wars were a series of three military conflicts between the British and French East India Companies for political supremacy in the Deccan and Coromandel Coast.",
                "First Carnatic War (1746–1748): An extension of the War of Austrian Succession in Europe; famous for the Battle of St. Thome (1746), where a small disciplined French force under Captain Paradise defeated Nawab Anwar-ud-din's large army; ended with the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748) restoring Madras to the British.",
                "Second Carnatic War (1749–1754): Initiated by Joseph François Dupleix's intervention in dynastic succession disputes of Hyderabad (Muzaffar Jung vs Nasir Jung) and Carnatic (Chanda Sahib vs Anwar-ud-din); turned by Robert Clive's audacious capture and 53-day siege of Arcot (1751); ended with Treaty of Pondicherry (1754) and recall of Dupleix.",
                "Third Carnatic War (1758–1763): An extension of the Seven Years' War; Count de Lally led the French; ended definitively with the Battle of Wandiwash (January 22, 1760), where Sir Eyre Coote crushed Lally; the Treaty of Paris (1763) reduced French settlements to unfortified trading posts."
            ],
