            "title": "Bengal on the Eve of British Conquest",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Riches and Autonomy of Bengal Subah</h3>\n<p>In the 18th century, the Subah of <strong>Bengal</strong> (encompassing modern West Bengal, Bangladesh, Bihar, and Odisha) was the agricultural powerhouse and manufacturing workshop of the Indian subcontinent. It produced vast surpluses of fine rice, muslin, silk, saltpetre, and indigo. European trading nations imported immense quantities of Bengal merchandise, paying primarily in silver bullion.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Nawabs of Independent Bengal</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Murshid Quli Khan (1717–1727):</strong> Appointed Diwan by Aurangzeb (1700) and Subahdar by Farrukhsiyar (1717). He transferred the provincial capital from Dhaka to <strong>Murshidabad</strong> (named after him). He restructured land revenue through <strong>Mal Zamini (revenue farming)</strong> and introduced the <em>Takkavi</em> (agricultural loans) system.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shuja-ud-Din (1727–1739):</strong> Son-in-law of Murshid Quli Khan; governed peacefully and annexed parts of Bihar.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sarfaraz Khan (1739–1740):</strong> Overthrown and killed by Alivardi Khan at the <strong>Battle of Giriya (1740)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Alivardi Khan (1740–1756):</strong> A capable military administrator who legitimized his usurpation by sending ₹2 crore to Mughal Emperor Muhammad Shah Rangila. He kept European trading companies strictly subordinate to state law, famously remarking: <em>'The Europeans are like a hive of bees; if you leave them alone, they will give you honey; if you disturb them, they will sting you to death.'</em> To stop devastating Maratha invasions (Bargi raids led by Raghuji Bhonsle of Nagpur), Alivardi ceded <strong>Orissa</strong> and agreed to pay ₹12 lakh annually as <em>Chauth</em> in 1751.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Siraj-ud-Daulah's Accession &amp; Domestic Rivalries (1756)</h3>\n<p>In April 1756, Alivardi Khan died, nominating his favorite grandson, <strong>Siraj-ud-Daulah</strong> (son of his youngest daughter Amina Begum and Zain-ud-Din), as his successor. The young Nawab's accession was immediately challenged by powerful family factions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ghaseti Begum:</strong> Siraj's maternal aunt (eldest daughter of Alivardi), possessing immense personal wealth.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shaukat Jung:</strong> Cousin of Siraj and Faujdar of Purnea, who claimed the throne.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mir Jafar:</strong> The Mir Bakshi (commander-in-chief of the Bengal army) and brother-in-law of Alivardi Khan, harboring royal ambitions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Landed and Merchant Magnates:</strong> The influential Marwari banking house of the <strong>Jagat Seths</strong> (led by Mahtab Rai and Swarup Chand), Rai Durlabh, and merchant Omichund, who resented Siraj's volatile temperament.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Triggers of Conflict with the English East India Company</h3>\n<p>Siraj-ud-Daulah demanded that European companies cease unauthorized fortifications and respect the authority of the Nawab. While the French at Chandernagore complied, the British at Fort William refused. The crisis exploded over three specific issues:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Abuse of Dastaks:</strong> Company servants routinely sold the royal trade passes (Dastaks) to private Indian merchants for personal kickbacks, severely defrauding the Nawab's treasury of legitimate customs duties.</li>\n  <li><strong>Illegal Fortifications:</strong> In anticipation of a Seven Years' War with the French, the British unilaterally began fortifying Fort William without the Nawab's permission. When ordered to demolish the new redoubts, British Governor Roger Drake insolently refused.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sheltering Political Absconders:</strong> The British granted political asylum at Fort William to <strong>Krishna Das</strong> (son of Diwan Rajballabh of Dhaka), who had fled with vast embezzled government funds, and arrogantly insulted the Nawab's envoy, Narayan Das.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Outraged by these open provocations, Siraj-ud-Daulah marched his army, captured the British factory at <strong>Kassim Bazar on June 4, 1756</strong>, and advanced directly upon Calcutta.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Bengal was the richest and most fertile province (Subah) of the Mughal Empire, celebrated as the 'Paradise of the Earth' (Jannat-ul-Bilad) for its immense agrarian revenue and booming textile, silk, and saltpetre exports.",
                "Murshid Quli Khan (1717–1727) was appointed Diwan and later Subahdar by Farrukhsiyar; he founded Murshidabad as the capital and introduced the Ijara (revenue farming) system.",
                "Alivardi Khan seized the Masnad of Bengal in 1740 after killing Sarfaraz Khan at the Battle of Giriya; he maintained strict control over European companies and bought peace from the Marathas (Raghuji Bhonsle) in 1751 by ceding Orissa and paying ₹12 lakh annual Chauth.",
                "Siraj-ud-Daulah succeeded his maternal grandfather Alivardi Khan in April 1756 at the age of 23, immediately encountering internal conspiracies from Ghaseti Begum, Shaukat Jung, and Mir Jafar."
            ],
