            "title": "Mir Jafar to Mir Qasim & Administrative Friction",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Mir Jafar's Troubled Reign (1757–1760)</h3>\n<p>Installed as the puppet Nawab after Plassey, <strong>Mir Jafar</strong> soon discovered that the East India Company's appetite for money was insatiable. Clive and his councilors extracted over ₹3 crore in gifts, salaries, and indemnities, completely draining the treasury. When Mir Jafar failed to meet subsequent extortionate financial demands, British officials looked for an excuse to replace him. Mir Jafar's secret overtures to the Dutch culminated in the Dutch defeat at the <strong>Battle of Bedara (1759)</strong>, sealing his fate.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Bloodless Coup of 1760 &amp; Mir Qasim's Rise</h3>\n<p>In 1760, Clive returned to England, and <strong>Henry Vansittart</strong> took charge as Governor of Bengal. Vansittart engineered a secret treaty with Mir Jafar's able and energetic son-in-law, <strong>Mir Qasim</strong>. Mir Jafar was forced to abdicate and retired to Calcutta on a pension of ₹1,500 per month.</p>\n<p>Under the <strong>Treaty of September 1760</strong>, Mir Qasim ceded the rich revenue districts of <strong>Burdwan, Midnapore, and Chittagong</strong> to the Company, gave ₹5 lakh to support the Company's war in the Carnatic, and paid massive personal gratuities to Vansittart and his councilors.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Mir Qasim's Reforms &amp; Modernization</h3>\n<p>Unlike his predecessor, Mir Qasim was a ruler of extraordinary administrative competence and patriotism who sought to restore sovereign authority:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Transfer of Capital to Munger (1762):</strong> To escape the intrigues and constant physical surveillance of Company agents at Murshidabad, Mir Qasim moved his capital 300 miles westward to <strong>Munger in Bihar</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modern Military Arsenal:</strong> At Munger, he established state-of-the-art foundries to cast bronze cannons and manufactured European-pattern firelocks (muskets). He employed European adventurers—most notably <strong>Walter Reinhard (popularly known as 'Sumroo')</strong> and an Armenian named <strong>Gorgin Khan (Gregory Arathoon)</strong>—to train his troops in modern infantry drills.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fiscal Reforms:</strong> He reclaimed arrears of land revenue, suppressed corrupt zamindars, dismissed disloyal officials, and levied an additional 1.5-anna cess (<em>Abwab</em>) to replenish the state treasury.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. The Flashpoint: Abolition of Internal Transit Duties (1763)</h3>\n<p>Company servants relentlessly abused the 1717 Farman by using <strong>Dastaks</strong> for their private trade in inland staples (salt, betel nut, tobacco), entirely evading the 25–40% customs duties paid by Indian merchants. This not only impoverished the Nawab's treasury but subjected native merchants to ruinous competition.</p>\n<p>When the Calcutta Council rejected a compromise negotiated by Vansittart and Warren Hastings, Mir Qasim executed a radical, egalitarian masterstroke: on <strong>March 22, 1763</strong>, he <strong>abolished all internal transit duties and tolls for all traders (Indian and European alike) for two years</strong>.</p>\n<p>This equalized trade conditions, destroying the illicit monopoly of the British. Outraged that Indian merchants were granted equal rights in their own country, the Calcutta Council declared war on Mir Qasim in July 1763. The British dispatched Major <strong>John Adams</strong>, who defeated Mir Qasim's forces in a series of engagements at <strong>Katwa, Giriya, and Sooty (1763)</strong>. Mir Qasim fled to Patna, where the infamous 'Patna Massacre' (execution of English prisoners by Sumroo) took place, before seeking refuge in Awadh.</p>\n",
