            "title": "The Battle of Buxar (October 22, 1764 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Formation of the Grand Tripartite Confederacy</h3>\n<p>After being expelled from Bihar in late 1763, <strong>Mir Qasim</strong> sought sanctuary with <strong>Nawab Shuja-ud-Daula</strong> of Awadh. At the time, Mughal Emperor <strong>Shah Alam II</strong> was also residing at Allahabad under Shuja-ud-Daula's protection. Mir Qasim persuaded both leaders to forge a grand military alliance to expel the British from Bengal, promising to finance the campaign with a daily subsidy of ₹11 lakh.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Engagement at Buxar (October 22, 1764)</h3>\n<p>The allied confederacy, numbering between <strong>40,000 and 50,000 troops</strong> with extensive cavalry and artillery, marched into Bihar and encamped near the fortified town of <strong>Buxar</strong> on the banks of the Ganga. The British forces were commanded by <strong>Major Hector Munro</strong>, comprising <strong>7,072 men</strong> (857 European soldiers, 5,297 drilled Indian sepoys, and 918 native cavalry) with 20 field pieces.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Course of Battle:</strong> The battle commenced on the morning of <strong>October 22, 1764</strong>. Shuja-ud-Daula's cavalry, supported by Afghan and Rohilla horsemen, launched ferocious charges against the British left flank. Munro deployed disciplined infantry square formations, maintaining a steady, devastating rolling musket fire and rapid grape shot from his artillery.</li>\n  <li><strong>Breakdown of the Allies:</strong> Lack of unified command crippled the Indian coalition. Mir Qasim took little active part; Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II secretly communicated with Munro, seeking British protection; and Shuja-ud-Daula's commanders fought without coordinated tactical cohesion. By early afternoon, the confederate lines broke. Over 2,000 allied soldiers perished, and 133 pieces of artillery were captured.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Decisive Consequences &amp; Military Comparison with Plassey</h3>\n<p>Historians universally consider Buxar far more momentous than Plassey:</p>\n<table class=\"table-module\">\n  <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Battle of Plassey (1757)</th><th>Battle of Buxar (1764)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Nature of Victory</strong></td><td>A victory achieved through secret treason, bribery, and palace coup</td><td>A decisive military triumph in open combat between disciplined armies</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Adversary Defeated</strong></td><td>Only the regional Nawab of Bengal (Siraj-ud-Daulah)</td><td>The Mughal Emperor (Shah Alam II), the Wazir of the Empire (Shuja-ud-Daula), and the Nawab of Bengal</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Geopolitical Scope</strong></td><td>Gave the Company a foothold in the Bengal Delta</td><td>Extended British hegemony over the entire Gangetic Valley up to Delhi</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Following the victory, British forces occupied Varanasi, Chunar, and Lucknow. Shuja-ud-Daula fled to Rohilkhand and surrendered unconditionally in May 1765. Emperor Shah Alam II placed himself directly under the protection of the East India Company.</p>\n",
