            "title": "The Dual Government in Bengal (1765–1772 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Structure of the Dual System (1765–1772)</h3>\n<p>Following the Grant of Diwani in 1765, Robert Clive introduced a novel administrative mechanism known as the <strong>Dual Government (Double Government)</strong>. Under traditional Mughal provincial administration, authority was bifurcated between two distinct offices:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Diwani:</strong> Collection of land revenue and civil justice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Nizamat:</strong> Military defense, police, public administration, and criminal justice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under Clive's arrangement, the East India Company acquired the <strong>Diwani</strong> directly from Emperor Shah Alam II and took over the <strong>Nizamat</strong> through a treaty with the minor Nawab Najm-ud-Daulah. However, the Company refused to assume the open direct administration of the province. Instead, it instituted a phantom administration:</p>\n<table class=\"table-module\">\n  <tr><th>Authority</th><th>Possession</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>East India Company</strong></td><td>All revenue collection, army, financial resources, ultimate power</td><td><strong>Zero Responsibility</strong> for governance, welfare, or police</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><strong>Puppet Nawab</strong></td><td>Zero army, zero treasury, subordinate to Company Resident</td><td><strong>Full Responsibility</strong> for peace, order, and famine relief</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>2. The Machinery of Naib Diwans</h3>\n<p>Because the Company lacked trained European revenue officers and feared arousing French and Dutch diplomatic protests or Indian peasant rebellion, Clive governed through native surrogates:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mohammad Reza Khan:</strong> Appointed <em>Naib Diwan</em> (Deputy Diwan) and <em>Naib Nazim</em> for Bengal, residing at Murshidabad.</li>\n  <li><strong>Raja Shitab Rai:</strong> Appointed <em>Naib Diwan</em> for Bihar, operating from Patna.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These Naib Diwans were tasked with squeezing every possible rupee out of the peasantry to satisfy the relentless demands of the Calcutta Council.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Catastrophic Repercussions</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ruin of Agriculture:</strong> Revenue collection was auctioned to the highest speculative bidder (farming system). Zamindars and revenue farmers engaged in ruthless extortion, causing peasants to abandon land.</li>\n  <li><strong>Destruction of Traditional Weavers:</strong> The Company's commercial agents (<em>Gomasthas</em>) forced native weavers to sign contracts at 30–40% below market value. Weavers who refused were flogged, imprisoned, or cut off their own thumbs to avoid forced weaving.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drain of Wealth:</strong> Massive private fortunes were siphoned out of Bengal by Company servants (termed 'Nabobs' in England), bankrupting the province.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. The Great Bengal Famine of 1770</h3>\n<p>In 1769, the monsoon rains failed across Bengal and Bihar, leading to severe crop failure. The Company not only refused to provide state grain relief or suspend revenue collection, but British officials cornered the rice market, hoarding grain and selling it at astronomical black-market prices. In 1770, the famine reached apocalyptic proportions: an estimated <strong>10 million people (one-third of Bengal's entire population)</strong> died of starvation and cholera. Richard Becher, a Company servant, confessed: <em>'The country of plenty is now becoming the scene of misery and famine.'</em> Shockingly, the Company collected more revenue in 1771 than in 1768.</p>\n\n<h3>5. Abolition by Warren Hastings (1772)</h3>\n<p>In 1772, <strong>Warren Hastings</strong> was appointed Governor of Bengal with strict instructions to dismantle the corrupt Dual System. Hastings arrested Mohammad Reza Khan and Raja Shitab Rai, abolished their offices, shifted the provincial treasury from Murshidabad to Calcutta, and placed the Company directly in charge of revenue administration through European District Collectors and a Board of Revenue.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Dual Government (Doidho Shashon) in Bengal was introduced by Robert Clive in 1765 and lasted until its abolition by Warren Hastings in 1772.",
                "Concept: Complete divorce of power from responsibility. The Company wielded absolute military power and fiscal revenue (Diwani), while the puppet Nawab held the judicial, police, and criminal responsibility (Nizamat) without funds or army.",
                "Clive appointed two Indian Deputy Diwans (Naib Diwans) to collect revenue: Mohammad Reza Khan for Bengal and Raja Shitab Rai for Bihar.",
                "Devastating Impact: Complete breakdown of law and order, ruin of agriculture and handicrafts, private extortion by Company agents, and the catastrophic Great Bengal Famine of 1770.",
                "The Great Bengal Famine (1770) wiped out nearly 10 million people (one-third of Bengal's population), yet the Company violently extorted revenue without remission."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "British statesman Edmund Burke condemned the Dual Government as 'a system of plunder and extortion under the guise of commercial enterprise.'",
                "Warren Hastings abolished the Dual System in 1772, terminated the allowances of Reza Khan and Shitab Rai, and brought the revenue administration directly under the Company's Board of Revenue."
            ],
