            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Background &amp; Causes of the Second War</h3>\n<p>The peace of 1769 was fragile. Two primary provocations sparked renewed conflict:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>British Breach of Faith (1771):</strong> When Peshwa Madhavrao invaded Mysore in 1771, Haidar Ali invoked the Treaty of Madras, requesting British military assistance. The Madras government perfidiously reneged, leaving Haidar to fight the Marathas alone. Haidar never forgave this betrayal.</li>\n  <li><strong>Seizure of Mahe (1779):</strong> When the American Revolutionary War (1775–1783) sparked war between Britain and France, the British captured French settlements in India. In March 1779, British troops seized <strong>Mahe</strong> (a French enclave on the Malabar coast). Mahe was situated inside Haidar's sovereign territory and was his primary conduit for importing French military hardware, guns, and ammunition. Haidar warned the British that an attack on Mahe would be treated as an act of war against Mysore. The British ignored his warning and seized the port.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>2. Outbreak &amp; The Catastrophe at Pollilur (1780)</h3>\n<p>In 1780, Haidar forged a formidable <strong>Grand Confederacy</strong> with the Marathas (embroiled in the First Anglo-Maratha War) and Nizam Ali of Hyderabad. In July 1780, Haidar swept down into the Carnatic with <strong>83,000 troops</strong> and 100 guns, laying siege to Arcot.</p>\n<p><strong>The Battle of Pollilur (September 10, 1780):</strong> British Colonel <strong>William Baillie</strong> was marching to link up with Sir Hector Munro's main army at Kanchipuram. Tipu Sultan and Haidar Ali intercepted Baillie at <strong>Pollilur</strong>. Mysorean rockets slammed into British ammunition tumbrils, causing massive explosions. The British force was annihilated: Baillie and 200 British soldiers were captured and imprisoned in the dungeons of Srirangapatna. Munro panicked, dumped his heavy artillery into the temple tank of Kanchipuram, and retreated in chaos to Madras. It was the most humiliating military catastrophe suffered by the British in India.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Warren Hastings' Counter-Offensive &amp; Porto Novo (1781)</h3>\n<p>Governor-General <strong>Warren Hastings</strong> reacted with frantic energy. He dispatched Bengal's best troops under veteran commander <strong>Sir Eyre Coote</strong> by sea to Madras, and used diplomacy to peel away Haidar's allies: he bribed the Raja of Berar (Bhonsle) and returned Guntur to the Nizam, shattering the confederacy.</p>\n<p>At the <strong>Battle of Porto Novo (Parangipettai) on July 1, 1781</strong>, Eyre Coote checked Haidar Ali's offensive in a hard-fought engagement. Coote defeated Haidar again at <strong>Pollilur (August 1781)</strong> and <strong>Sholinghur (September 1781)</strong>, although British forces remained unable to conquer inland Mysore.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Death of Haidar Ali &amp; The Treaty of Mangalore (1784)</h3>\n<p>On <strong>December 7, 1782</strong>, Haidar Ali died of a cancerous growth (carbuncle) in his camp near Chittoor at the age of 60. His son, the 32-year-old <strong>Tipu Sultan</strong>, assumed leadership and continued the war with ferocious vigor.</p>\n<p>Tipu captured <strong>Bednur</strong> and laid siege to <strong>Mangalore</strong>, trapping British General John Campbell. With the signing of the Treaty of Versailles (1783) ending the American War, French forces under Bussy withdrew from supporting Tipu. Facing financial exhaustion, Lord Macartney (Governor of Madras) defied Warren Hastings and negotiated directly with Tipu.</p>\n<p>On <strong>March 11, 1784</strong>, the <strong>Treaty of Mangalore</strong> was signed:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Both sides agreed to <strong>mutual restitution of all conquered territories</strong>.</li>\n  <li>All surviving prisoners of war (including British soldiers and officers) were released.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Warren Hastings was so infuriated by the treaty's equal terms that he exclaimed: <em>'What a man is this Lord Macartney! I yet believe that in spite of the peace, he will effect the loss of the Carnatic!'</em></p>\n",
