            "title": "Third Anglo-Mysore War (1790–1792 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Causes of the Third Anglo-Mysore War</h3>\n<p>Following the Treaty of Mangalore, <strong>Tipu Sultan</strong> modernized his state, reformed land revenue, established state trading monopolies, and rebuilt his army. In 1789, a boundary dispute arose with the <strong>Raja of Travancore</strong> (a British ally protected by the 1784 treaty). The Raja purchased two Dutch forts—<strong>Cranganore and Ayacotta</strong>—which Tipu claimed were within his tributary territory. In <strong>April 1790</strong>, Tipu launched an offensive against Travancore's fortified border wall (the Travancore Lines). Governor-General <strong>Lord Cornwallis</strong> declared war on Mysore.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Cornwallis's Triple Alliance</h3>\n<p>Cornwallis took meticulous diplomatic precautions to isolate Tipu. He concluded offensive-defensive treaties with the <strong>Marathas (June 1790)</strong> and the <strong>Nizam of Hyderabad (July 1790)</strong>, guaranteeing them equal shares in the conquered territories of Mysore.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Military Campaigns &amp; The Siege of Srirangapatna</h3>\n<p>Initial British campaigns under General Medows faltered against Tipu's mobile defense. In <strong>1791</strong>, Cornwallis personally assumed supreme field command. Advancing from Madras, Cornwallis captured the formidable fortress of <strong>Bangalore (March 1791)</strong> and marched on the island fortress of <strong>Srirangapatna</strong>. Although monsoons and supply starvation forced a temporary retreat, Cornwallis returned in early <strong>1792</strong> with allied Maratha and Nizam armies, completely encircling Srirangapatna. Recognizing that further resistance would result in the storming and sack of his capital, Tipu sued for peace.</p>\n\n<h3>4. The Humiliating Treaty of Srirangapatna (March 1792)</h3>\n<p>Signed on <strong>March 18, 1792</strong>, the treaty imposed crippling conditions on Mysore:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Territorial Dismemberment:</strong> Tipu ceded <strong>half of his entire kingdom</strong> to the allies:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>British Share:</strong> <strong>Malabar</strong> (securing coastal pepper trade), <strong>Dindigul</strong>, and <strong>Baramahal</strong> (modern Salem and Coimbatore districts, guarding passes into the Mysore plateau).</li>\n      <li><strong>Maratha Share:</strong> Territories extending up to the <strong>Tungabhadra River</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Nizam's Share:</strong> Lands extending from the <strong>Krishna to beyond the Pennar River</strong> (including Cuddapah and Gandikota).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>War Indemnity:</strong> Tipu was compelled to pay an astronomical indemnity of <strong>₹3.3 crore (30 million rupees)</strong>. He paid ₹1.65 crore immediately and surrendered <strong>two of his young sons (Abdul Khaliq and Muiz-ud-din)</strong> as hostages to Lord Cornwallis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Release of Prisoners:</strong> All European prisoners dating back to Haidar Ali's wars were released.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cornwallis proudly summed up his strategic triumph to the Court of Directors: <em>'We have effectively crippled our enemy without making our friends too formidable.'</em> Tipu paid the balance of indemnity within two years, redeeming his hostage sons, but harbored an unquenchable resolve to avenge his humiliation.</p>\n",
