            "title": "The Anglo-Maratha Wars: First & Second Anglo-Maratha Wars",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Structure of the Maratha Confederacy</h3>\n<p>Following the Third Battle of Panipat (1761), the centralized authority of the <strong>Peshwa</strong> waned, evolving into a loose <strong>Maratha Confederacy</strong> of five autonomous dynasties:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Peshwa</strong> at Poona (nominal executive head)</li>\n  <li><strong>Scindia (Sindhia)</strong> at Gwalior (preeminent military power under Mahadji Scindia)</li>\n  <li><strong>Holkar</strong> at Indore (Malhar Rao and later Jaswant Rao Holkar)</li>\n  <li><strong>Bhonsle</strong> at Nagpur and Berar</li>\n  <li><strong>Gaekwad</strong> at Baroda</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>2. First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–1782)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cause:</strong> In 1773, young Peshwa Narayan Rao was assassinated at the instigation of his uncle <strong>Raghunath Rao (Raghoba)</strong>. A council of twelve ministers known as the <strong>Barabhai</strong> (headed by <strong>Nana Fadnavis</strong> and <strong>Mahadji Scindia</strong>) proclaimed Narayan Rao's posthumous infant son, <strong>Madhavrao II (Sawai Madhavrao)</strong>, as the legal Peshwa. Desperate for power, Raghoba fled to the British at Bombay and signed the <strong>Treaty of Surat (1775)</strong>, ceding Salsette and Bassein in exchange for military support.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conflict &amp; Wadgaon:</strong> Warren Hastings sent an army from Bengal. In January 1779, Maratha cavalry trapped the British Bombay army in the Western Ghats passes at <strong>Wadgaon</strong>. The British were compelled to sign the humiliating <strong>Convention of Wadgaon (1779)</strong>, surrendering all conquered territories. Hastings repudiated Wadgaon, dispatched General Goddard (who took Ahmedabad and Bassein), while Captain Popham stormed the impregnable fort of <strong>Gwalior (1780)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Treaty of Salbai (May 17, 1782):</strong> Mediated by Mahadji Scindia, the Treaty of Salbai restored peace:\n    <ul>\n      <li>Madhavrao II was recognized as legitimate Peshwa; Raghoba was pensioned off.</li>\n      <li>The British retained <strong>Salsette</strong>, restoring other conquests.</li>\n      <li>Secured <strong>20 years of peace</strong>, allowing the British to crush Mysore.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–1805)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Internal Anarchy:</strong> The deaths of Mahadji Scindia (1794) and Nana Fadnavis (1800, 'the last of the Maratha statesmen') left Poona in the hands of the weak and treacherous Peshwa <strong>Baji Rao II</strong>. In October 1802, <strong>Jaswant Rao Holkar</strong> defeated the combined armies of Peshwa Baji Rao II and Daulat Rao Scindia at the <strong>Battle of Hadapsar (Poona)</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Treaty of Bassein (December 31, 1802):</strong> Baji Rao II fled to the British at Bassein and signed a total surrender: he accepted Lord Wellesley's <strong>Subsidiary Alliance</strong>, agreed to station a British force of 6,000 troops at Poona, ceded territories worth ₹26 lakh annually, surrendered foreign policy, and agreed to dismiss all non-British Europeans.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Outbreak of War:</strong> Stung by the Peshwa's betrayal, Daulat Rao Scindia and Raghuji Bhonsle joined forces to fight the British. Holkar and Gaekwad remained aloof due to internal feuds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Major Battles:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Deccan Theatre:</strong> <strong>General Arthur Wellesley</strong> won brilliant, bloody victories at the <strong>Battle of Assaye (September 1803)</strong> and <strong>Argaon (November 1803)</strong>, storming Gawilgarh.</li>\n      <li><strong>Hindustan Theatre:</strong> <strong>General Lord Gerard Lake</strong> routed Scindia's French-drilled army at Aligarh and Delhi (September 1803), took Emperor Shah Alam II under British custody, and won the <strong>Battle of Laswari (November 1803)</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Treaties of Submission:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Treaty of Deogaon (Dec 1803):</strong> Bhonsle ceded Cuttack (uniting Bengal and Madras) and Balasore.</li>\n      <li><strong>Treaty of Surji-Anjangaon (Dec 1803):</strong> Scindia ceded the Ganga-Yamuna Doab, Delhi, Agra, and parts of Gujarat.</li>\n      <li><strong>Treaty of Rajghat (1805):</strong> Holkar (who later took up arms and besieged Delhi) signed peace under Governor-General George Barlow.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Maratha Confederacy consisted of five powerful houses: (1) Peshwa of Poona, (2) Scindia (Sindhia) of Gwalior, (3) Holkar of Indore, (4) Bhonsle of Nagpur/Berar, and (5) Gaekwad of Baroda.",
                "First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–1782): Sparked by the Treaty of Surat (1775) signed between Bombay Government and ambitious claimant Raghunath Rao (Raghoba); opposed by Nana Fadnavis and the Barabhai council under infant Peshwa Madhavrao II.",
                "Convention of Wadgaon (1779): Maratha forces under Mahadji Scindia surrounded and humiliated the British army; war concluded with the Treaty of Salbai (May 17, 1782), securing 20 years of peace and mutual restitution.",
                "Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–1805): Peshwa Baji Rao II fled Poona after being defeated by Jaswant Rao Holkar at the Battle of Hadapsar (1802), signing the humiliating Treaty of Bassein (Dec 31, 1802) with Wellesley, accepting the Subsidiary Alliance.",
                "Arthur Wellesley defeated Scindia and Bhonsle at Assaye and Argaon (1803), while Lord Lake captured Delhi and Agra (Battle of Laswari); ended with Treaties of Deogaon (Bhonsle) and Surji-Anjangaon (Scindia)."
            ],
