            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Sikh Empire under Maharaja Ranjit Singh (1799–1839)</h3>\n<p>Born into the <strong>Sukerchakia Misl</strong> in 1780, <strong>Maharaja Ranjit Singh</strong> unified the twelve autonomous Sikh misls. He captured <strong>Lahore in 1799</strong> from the Bhangi misl (making it his capital) and acquired the holy city of <strong>Amritsar in 1802</strong>. Under the <strong>Treaty of Amritsar (1809)</strong> with British envoy <strong>Charles Metcalfe</strong>, Ranjit Singh accepted the <strong>Sutlej River</strong> as the eastern boundary of his kingdom.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Fauj-i-Khas:</strong> Ranjit Singh built a formidable modern standing army. He hired French and Italian Napoleonic veterans—notably <strong>Jean-François Allard</strong> (cavalry), <strong>Jean-Baptiste Ventura</strong> (infantry), <strong>Claude Auguste Court</strong>, and <strong>Paolo Avitabile</strong>—to drill his soldiers, casting state-of-the-art bronze artillery at Lahore.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conquests:</strong> Annexed Multan (1818), Kashmir (1819), and Peshawar (1834), and acquired the legendary <strong>Koh-i-Noor diamond</strong> from Afghan monarch Shah Shuja.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Decade of Anarchy &amp; First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–1846)</h3>\n<p>After Ranjit Singh's death in June 1839, the Lahore Durbar descended into palace murders and factional intrigue between Dogra brothers (Gulab Singh, Dhian Singh) and the Sandhanwalia Sardars. In 1843, Ranjit Singh's five-year-old son, <strong>Maharaja Duleep Singh</strong>, was proclaimed ruler with his mother, <strong>Rani Jindan</strong>, as Regent.</p>\n<p>In December 1845, provoked by British troop buildups on the frontier, the Khalsa army crossed the Sutlej River. The British Governor-General <strong>Lord Hardinge</strong> declared war.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Treason of Commanders:</strong> The brave soldiers of the Khalsa were systematically betrayed by their own corrupt commanders: Prime Minister <strong>Lal Singh</strong> and Commander-in-Chief <strong>Tej Singh</strong>, who secretly communicated tactical troop movements and artillery dispositions to British commanders.</li>\n  <li><strong>Engagements:</strong> Fierce battles took place at <strong>Mudki</strong>, <strong>Ferozeshah</strong> (where the British army teetered on the brink of defeat), and <strong>Aliwal</strong> (under Sir Harry Smith).</li>\n  <li><strong>Battle of Sobraon (February 10, 1846):</strong> The decisive battle on the banks of the Sutlej. When Tej Singh destroyed the bridge of boats and fled, thousands of Sikh soldiers refused to surrender, fighting heroically under veteran <strong>Sardar Sham Singh Attariwalla</strong> until annihilated in the river.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. The Treaties of Lahore &amp; Amritsar (March 1846)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Lahore (March 8, 1846):</strong> Punjab ceded the rich <strong>Jalandhar Doab</strong> (between Beas and Sutlej); the Sikh army was drastically reduced to 20,000 infantry and 12,000 cavalry; an indemnity of ₹1.5 crore was demanded.</li>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Amritsar (March 16, 1846):</strong> Unable to pay ₹1 crore of the indemnity, the Durbar ceded Jammu and Kashmir. The British sold Kashmir directly to Dogra chief <strong>Raja Gulab Singh</strong> for <strong>₹75 lakh</strong>, creating the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir.</li>\n  <li><strong>Treaty of Bhairowal (December 1846):</strong> Rani Jindan was removed from the regency on a pension of ₹48,000; a Council of Regency was established under British Resident <strong>Henry Lawrence</strong>, effectively placing Punjab under British administration.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Second Anglo-Sikh War &amp; Annexation (1848–1849)</h3>\n<p>Deep resentment against British meddling erupted into widespread rebellion:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Spark:</strong> In April 1848, <strong>Dewan Mulraj</strong> (Governor of Multan) rebelled when replaced by the British, and two British officers (Vans Agnew and Anderson) were killed. In the northwest, <strong>Chattar Singh Attariwalla</strong> and his son <strong>Sher Singh</strong> raised the standard of open revolt.</li>\n  <li><strong>Military Clashes:</strong> Lord Dalhousie seized the opportunity to annex the entire province. At the <strong>Battle of Chillianwala (January 13, 1849)</strong>, Sher Singh's artillery inflicted catastrophic casualties on General Hugh Gough's army. Gough was superseded, but before his successor arrived, Gough retrieved his reputation at the <strong>Battle of Gujarat ('The Battle of Guns', February 21, 1849)</strong>, routing the Sikh and Afghan artillery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On <strong>March 29, 1849</strong>, <strong>Lord Dalhousie</strong> issued a proclamation formally annexing Punjab to the British Empire. Young Maharaja Duleep Singh was deposed, pensioned off, and sent to England; the <strong>Koh-i-Noor diamond</strong> was confiscated and presented to Queen Victoria. Punjab was placed under a triumvirate <strong>Board of Administration</strong> consisting of <strong>Henry Lawrence</strong> (President), <strong>John Lawrence</strong>, and <strong>Charles Mansel</strong>.</p>\n",
